e grilling.md, then `go build` will produce a binary WITHOUT grilling embedded. But the Python test script would need to:\n1. Back up grilling.md\n2. Delete grilling.md\n3. Rebuild (go build)\n4. Run a Go program/test that calls BuildPlan\n5. Check it returns error containing \"grilling\"\n6. Restore grilling.md\n7. Rebuild again (restore original state)\n\nThis is complex and mutates the repo. The test script should be idempotent. It needs to restore state afterward.\n\nHmm, but actually there's a subtlety about how to test the exception case cleanly. Let me think about what \"run PIBuilder.BuildPlan\" means in the Python test context.\n\nThe most robust way is:\n1. Create a temporary Go test file (or a standalone main.go in a temp dir that's part of the module) that:\n   - Imports `github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/builder`\n   - Calls `NewPIBuilder().BuildPlan(\"test requirement\", map[string]string{...})`\n   - Prints the result/error to stdout in a structured way.\n\nActually, simpler: use `go test` with a temp test file. But the temp test file needs to be in the module (internal/builder package) to access unexported things — but BuildPlan is exported, so it can be in any package within the module that imports internal/builder. Since internal packages can only be imported by code within the same module root (github.com/sunquan/rick/...), we can create a temp directory inside the repo, e.g., under `internal/` or root, with a main.go that imports the builder.\n\nActually the simplest is to write a small Go program into a temp dir INSIDE the repo (e.g., a subdirectory), run `go run`, then remove it. But `go run` of a file that imports internal/builder requires the file to be within the module `github.com/sunquan/rick`. So we can create a temp dir like `/workdir/.../rick/.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/tests/tmp_probe/main.go`? No — that's within the repo but is it within the module? The module is `github.com/sunquan/rick` with go.mod at repo root. Any Go file within the re