{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The convention is to build the binary to a temp directory (not pollute bin/rick). But the task10 test method explicitly says `./bin/rick plan --dry-run`. \n\nHmm, there's a tension. The test method literally says `./bin/rick plan --dry-run`. But the convention from other tests is to build to temp. However, the task10 test method is explicit about `./bin/rick`.\n\nLet me think about what's most correct. The test method's operation uses `./bin/rick`. The precondition says \"build 成功\" (build success). So the intent is: after a successful build, `./bin/rick plan --dry-run` should produce the inlined output.\n\nFor the test script to be accurate and idempotent, and to not rely on a potentially stale `bin/rick`, I have two options:\n1. Build a fresh binary to a temp path, then run `<tmp>/rick plan --dry-run` instead of `./bin/rick`.\n2. Use `./bin/rick` directly as-is.\n\nThe test method explicitly says `./bin/rick plan --dry-run`. But it also says \"build 成功\" as precondition. To satisfy both, I could rebuild `bin/rick` in place (`go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick`) at the start, then use `./bin/rick`. But that pollutes the tracked binary (it's git-tracked? bin/rick is tracked per git status — actually the earlier git status didn't show bin/rick as modified, meaning it's either tracked-and-clean or untracked-and-ignored. Let me check .gitignore).\n\nActually, the previous tests deliberately avoid polluting bin/rick. But for task10, the test method literally says `./bin/rick`. \n\nLet me reconcile: The cleanest, most faithful approach is to build a FRESH binary to a temp location and run `<tmp>/rick plan --dry-run`, because:\n1. It tests the CURRENT source code (idempotent, deterministic).\n2. It doesn't rely on stale bin/rick.\n3. It doesn't pollute the repo.\n\nBut the test method says `./bin/rick`. However, the spirit is: the CLI binary `rick` run with `plan --dry-run`. Using a freshly-built binary is equivalent and more correct.\n\nHmm, but wait. Actually there's a deeper consideration. Let me re-read the test method carefully:\n\n\"正常路径：前置条件 = task5 完成；输入 = `plan --dry-run`；操作 = `./bin/rick plan --dry-run | wc -l` + `./bin/rick plan --dry-run | grep -c '{{'`；预期 = 输出为单个连贯 prompt（行数 > 0），`{{` 计数 0。\"\n\n\"边界（单文件内聚）：前置条件 = build 成功；输入 = `plan --dry-run`；操作 = `./bin/rick plan --dry-run | grep -cE 'Grilling|结构化追问'`；预期 = ≥1\"\n\nThe phrase \"前置条件 = build 成功\" strongly implies the test should ensure a successful build first (i.e., build the binary). And \"`./bin/rick`\" implies the binary is at bin/rick.\n\nI think the best approach that faithfully implements the test method AND follows the established convention is:\n- Build the binary to a temp location (or to bin/rick) as the \"build 成功\" precondition.\n- Run the built binary with `plan --dry-run`.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. I'll build to a temp dir to avoid polluting, but ALSO the test method says `./bin/rick`. To be safe and faithful, I'll build to `bin/rick`? No.\n\nLet me look at how the actual existing `bin/rick` was last built — it's from Aug 17 04:50 (recent). The tasks.json is being worked on. Let me check the .gitignore to see if bin/rick is tracked."}}
