other issue: the temp Go program for test 3 imports the SAME `internal/builder` package that the normal/boundary tests use via the `bin/rick` binary. But normal/boundary use a pre-built binary (temp-built), while test 3 uses `go run`. These are separate compile units. Fine.\n\nNow, the temp Go program's behavior: after task10 is implemented, `BuildPlan` will call `LoadCoreSkills` (or similar) to inline grilling. If grilling.md is deleted, the embedded FS won't have it, and BuildPlan should return error \"grilling ... not found\".\n\nBut currently (task10 not implemented), BuildPlan does NOT load grilling. So even with grilling.md deleted, BuildPlan returns success (no error). So test 3 currently fails → RED. Good.\n\nLet me design the temp Go program to output a clear signal:\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n    \"fmt\"\n    \"os\"\n\n    \"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/builder\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n    _, _, err := builder.NewPIBuilder().BuildPlan(\"test requirement\", map[string]string{\n        \"rick_dir\": os.Getenv(\"RICK_DIR\"),\n        \"job_plan_dir\": os.Getenv(\"JOB_PLAN_DIR\"),\n    })\n    if err != nil {\n        fmt.Printf(\"ERROR: %v\\n\", err)\n        os.Exit(0)  // error is the EXPECTED outcome; we print it and exit 0\n    }\n    fmt.Println(\"NO_ERROR\")\n}\n```\n\nWait, I need to think about how the Python script interprets this. The expected behavior (after task10) is: error returned with \"grilling\" in message. So:\n- If output contains \"ERROR:\" and \"grilling\" → test 3 passes.\n- If output is \"NO_ERROR\" → test 3 fails (silent success, no error).\n- If the program panics or exits non-zero → test 3 fails (with a different message).\n\nActually, let me structure the Go program to print a clear machine-readable result. Let me have it print to stdout:\n- On error: `err=grilling ...` (print the error)\n- On no error: `ok`\n\nAnd always exit 0 (unless it panics). Then Python checks:\n- If the program crashes (non-zero exit) → fail \"BuildPlan panicked instead of returning error\".\n- If output contains the error marker AND \"grilling\" → pass.\n- Else → fail.\n\nActually, let me make the Go program more explicit and robust. I'll print:\n- `{\"has_error\": true, \"msg\": \"...\"}` — no, simpler: just print the error string or \"NO_ERROR\".\n\nLet me write the Go program:\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\n\t\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/builder\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n\trickDir := os.Getenv(\"RICK_TASK10_RICK_DIR\")\n\tjobPlanDir := os.Getenv(\"RICK_TASK10_JOB_PLAN_DIR\")\n\t_, _, err := builder.NewPIBuilder().BuildPlan(\"dry-run requirement\", map[string]string{\n\t\t\"rick_dir\":     rickDir,\n\t\t\"job_plan_dir\": jobPlanDir,\n\t})\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Printf(\"ERR:%v\\n\", err)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tfmt.Println(\"NO_ERROR\")\n}\n```\n\nPython checks:\n- rc == 0 (no panic)\n- stdout contains \"ERR:\" \n- stdout contains \"grilling\" (case-insensitive)\n\nIf all → pass. Else fail with appropriate message.\n\nNow, there's a question about whether `go run` needs the rick_dir to be valid. BuildPlan uses `prompt.LoadLoopsContext(filepath.Join(rickDir, \"loops\"))`. If rickDir is empty/invalid, LoadLoopsContext would just return empty (it handles non-existent dir). Actually let me check LoadLoopsContext behavior — it reads the dir and logs warnings. It won't error. So rickDir can be the repo's .rick dir or anything. Let me pass the actual repo root's .rick dir or just repo root. Actually `rick_dir` is used as `filepath.Join(rickDir, \"loops\")` and `filepath.Join(rickDir, \"domain\")` and `filepath.Join(rickDir, \"jobs\", \"job_N\", \"plan\")` (for job_plan_dir if empty). Let me pass rickDir = repo_root/.rick (the actual .rick dir) so LoadLoopsContext finds real loops. Actually it doesn't matter for the error test; but to be safe and realistic, pass repo_root/.rick.\n\nActually, wait — for the exception test, the key is that BuildPlan returns error containing \"grilling\"