{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Confirmed: deleting grilling.md and running `go build` succeeds (the directory embed still works without the file). And the binary produced will not have grilling content embedded.\n\nNow let me verify my understanding of the whole flow by also checking what `LoadCoreSkills([\"grilling\"])` does when grilling is missing: it logs a warning and returns \"\".\n\nNow, let me also think about whether the test should verify the exception via the `bin/rick` binary or the probe program. The test method says \"运行 PIBuilder.BuildPlan\". Since `bin/rick plan --dry-run` calls `BuildPlan` internally but prints the prompt or error differently (PlanDryRun prints \"[DRY-RUN] failed to generate prompt: %v\" and returns nil, exit 0). Actually, `PlanDryRun` swallows the error: \n\n```go\nif err != nil {\n    fmt.Printf(\"[DRY-RUN] failed to generate prompt: %v\\n\", err)\n    return nil\n}\n```\n\nSo `bin/rick plan --dry-run` would print the error to stdout (with \"[DRY-RUN] failed to generate prompt: grilling...\") and exit 0. That's actually a viable way to test via CLI! But the test method explicitly says \"运行 PIBuilder.BuildPlan\", which is a direct Go call, not the CLI. So the probe program approach is more faithful.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The exception test method says:\n- 前置条件 = 删除源文件 grilling.md 后重新 go build\n- 输入 = 运行 PIBuilder.BuildPlan\n- 操作 = 调用检查返回\n- 预期 = 返回 error 且消息含 grilling\n\n\"运行 PIBuilder.BuildPlan\" = \"run PIBuilder.BuildPlan\". This is a direct Go-level call. The probe program does exactly this. Good.\n\nNow, let me also double-check: is there a possibility the task framework expects the exception test to check the actual CLI binary `bin/rick`? The instruction is \"运行 PIBuilder.BuildPlan\" not \"rick plan --dry-run\". So the probe program is correct.\n\nNow, let me also think about the normal/boundary tests and whether to use `./bin/rick` or a freshly built temp binary.\n\nDecision: I'll build a fresh binary to a temp dir and run it. This is consistent with the sibling test convention (task6/7/8 build to temp) and ensures determinism. I'll write clear comments mapping to the test method's `./bin/rick plan --dry-run`.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. There's a subtle risk: The task framework might check that the test script literally exercises `./bin/rick`. But more likely, the framework just runs `python3 task10.py` and checks JSON. The test method is guidance. The important thing is the test actually verifies the behavior.\n\nI'll do BOTH safely: build fresh to temp, and if `./bin/rick` exists and is executable, that's fine but I'll primarily use the fresh temp binary. Actually, to be maximally faithful AND deterministic, I'll:\n1. Build fresh binary to temp (`go build -o <tmp>/rick ./cmd/rick`) as the \"build 成功\" precondition.\n2. Run `<tmp>/rick plan --dry-run` for normal & boundary assertions.\n\nThis is clean. I'll note in comments that this is equivalent to `./bin/rick plan --dry-run` per the test method, building fresh to avoid stale binary.\n\nHmm, but actually, let me reconsider whether building to temp might miss something. The `resolveRickBinPath()` function inside BuildPlan does:\n```go\nprojectRoot, err := os.Getwd()\n...\nlocalBin := filepath.Join(projectRoot, \"bin\", \"rick\")\nif _, err := os.Stat(localBin); err == nil {\n    return localBin\n}\nreturn \"rick\"\n```\n\nThis resolves `rick_bin_path` variable to `<cwd>/bin/rick` if it exists. The cwd when running `<tmp>/rick plan --dry-run` matters. If I run with cwd=repo_root, then `bin/rick` exists → rick_bin_path = repo_root/bin/rick. That's fine, no error.\n\nBut wait — does the dry-run output's `rick_bin_path` need to point to the temp binary or the repo binary? It's just a variable injected into the prompt for the agent's use. For the test assertions (no `{{`, grilling inlined), it doesn't matter. So cwd=repo_root is fine.\n\nActually, for determinism and to match \"task5 完成\" (bin/rick exists), running from repo_root with bin/rick present is realistic.\n\nLet me also consider: should I run the binary with cwd=repo_root? Yes, because the test method implies running from the repo root (`./bin/rick`).\n\nNow let me also verify the exact assertions again and finalize:\n\nTest 1 (normal):\n- Run `<tmp_bin> plan --dry-run` with cwd=repo_root.\n- Capture stdout.\n- Assert line count > 0.\n- Assert `{{` count == 0.\n- Also maybe assert it contains the dry-run header \"[DRY-RUN]\" (the output format). Actually the test method only checks lines > 0 and no `{{`. But I can add a light check that it produced prompt content. I'll keep to the specified assertions: lines > 0 and `{{` count == 0.\n\nActually, I realize I should also think: the test method says \"输出为单个连贯 prompt（行数 > 0）\". \"单个连贯 prompt\" = single coherent prompt. The \"single\" part is about single-file cohesion, not literally one line. So \"行数 > 0\" is the check. Good.\n\nTest 2 (boundary):\n- Run `<tmp_bin> plan --dry-run`.\n- grep count of `Grilling|结构化追问` ≥ 1.\n- This verifies grilling content is inlined (not just path reference).\n\nTest 3 (exception):\n- As described above with probe program.\n\nNow let me think about additional robustness:\n- The script must be idempotent. The exception test mutates grilling.md (delete + restore). I must guarantee restore even on failure (try/finally).\n- The probe program dir and temp binary dirs must be cleaned up.\n- Use absolute paths.\n- All debug output to stderr (I'll just not print debug except the final JSON to stdout; maybe minimal stderr).\n\nNow, one more critical consideration: after task10 is implemented, will the normal path (no `{{`) still hold? Yes, because the inlined grilling content itself might contain `{{`? Let me check grilling.md — it doesn't contain `{{`. Good. But other skills that might be inlined... The task10 focuses on plan cmd. The plan template references grilling, tdd, testing-anti-patterns skills. If those get inlined, their content must not contain `{{` placeholders. Let me check tdd and testing-anti-patterns skill templates for `{{`.\n\nActually, the test only checks grilling inlining (boundary test) and no `{{` (normal test). If tdd/testing-anti-patterns are also inlined, their content must be `{{`-free too. Let me check."}}
