{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Good. The skill files don't contain `{{` placeholders, so inlining them won't introduce `{{`. The grilling.md contains \"Grilling\" (3 times) and \"结构化追问\" content.\n\nNow I have everything I need. Let me also double check the plan template's current grilling reference and how task10 might change it. The boundary test greps for \"Grilling|结构化追问\" in the OUTPUT. After inlining, the grilling.md content (\"Skill: Grilling（结构化追问协议）\" and \"结构化追问\" throughout) will appear in output. Currently, the output has \"grilling 追问\" (lowercase) but not \"Grilling\" (capital) or \"结构化追问\". Confirmed count = 0.\n\nNow let me write the test script. I'll model it after task9.py conventions.\n\nLet me carefully design the script structure:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"task10 验收测试...\"\"\"\nimport json\nimport os\nimport shutil\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport tempfile\n\n# Go probe program that calls PIBuilder.BuildPlan directly\n_PROBE_GO = '''package main\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\n\t\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/builder\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n\trickDir := os.Getenv(\"RICK_TASK10_RICK_DIR\")\n\tjobPlanDir := os.Getenv(\"RICK_TASK10_JOB_PLAN_DIR\")\n\t_, _, err := builder.NewPIBuilder().BuildPlan(\"dry-run requirement\", map[string]string{\n\t\t\"rick_dir\":     rickDir,\n\t\t\"job_plan_dir\": jobPlanDir,\n\t})\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Printf(\"ERR:%v\\\\n\", err)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tfmt.Println(\"NO_ERROR\")\n}\n'''\n```\n\nWait, there's a subtlety with the `map[string]string` literal — in Go, `map[string]string{\"rick_dir\": rickDir, ...}` is valid. Good.\n\nFunctions:\n- find_repo_root(start_file) — same as task9.\n- run(cmd, cwd, timeout, env) — same.\n- tail(text, n) — same.\n- write_file(path, content) — same.\n- read_text(path) — same.\n\nThen main():\n1. repo_root = find_repo_root(__file__)\n2. go = shutil.which('go'); if not found, output fail JSON.\n3. Build fresh binary to temp: `go build -o <tmp>/rick ./cmd/rick` with cwd=repo_root. If fail, append error.\n4. Normal path: run `<tmp>/rick plan --dry-run` with cwd=repo_root, capture stdout. \n   - lines = stdout.count('\\n') + (1 if stdout and not stdout.endswith('\\n') else 0). Actually simpler: count lines = len(stdout.splitlines()). Use that.\n   - assert lines > 0.\n   - count '{{' == 0.\n5. Boundary: run again (or reuse stdout) → count of regex `Grilling|结构化追问` ≥ 1. I'll use a Python regex search over stdout, count matches. Use re.findall(r'Grilling|结构化追问', stdout). Count >= 1.\n6. Exception: \n   - write probe program to `.tmp_task10_probe_*` dir in repo_root.\n   - backup grilling.md → delete → rebuild probe → run probe → restore grilling.md.\n   - Use try/finally for restore.\n   \n   Actually, careful: the probe needs rick_dir and job_plan_dir. rick_dir = repo_root/.rick? Actually BuildPlan uses rickDir for `filepath.Join(rickDir, \"loops\")` and `filepath.Join(rickDir, \"domain\")`. The real rick dir is repo_root/.rick. Let me check workspace.GetRickDir returns... Actually the handler PlanDryRun uses `workspace.GetRickDir()`. Let me check what that returns.\n\nLet me check workspace.GetRickDir to pass the right rick_dir.\n\nActually, for the exception test, the rick_dir just needs to be a directory that exists (so LoadLoopsContext doesn't fail — though it handles non-existent gracefully). It doesn't affect the grilling error. I'll pass repo_root/.rick as rick_dir (the actual .rick dir), and job_plan_dir = repo_root/.rick/jobs/job_N/plan. Or I can just pass empty and let BuildPlan default. But to be realistic, I'll compute rick_dir = os.path.join(repo_root, '.rick').\n\nActually, let me check: does `.rick` dir exist? Yes (repo has .rick). And it has loops/ and domain/ subdirs? Let me verify .rick structure.\n\nActually earlier ls showed .rick exists with domain, dream, jobs, loops, etc. So repo_root/.rick has loops and domain. Good.\n\nFor the probe's BuildPlan call, I'll set:\n- rick_dir = repo_root/.rick\n- job_plan_dir = repo_root/.rick/jobs/job_N/plan\n\nBut wait — I need to be careful: BuildPlan's error for missing grilling. After task10, the implementation might load grilling from embedded FS (LoadCoreSkills). This is independent of rick_dir. So rick_dir value doesn't matter for the grilling error. Fine.\n\nNow, about building the probe: `go build -o <probe_bin> .` with cwd = probe_dir. The probe_dir is inside repo_root (e.g., repo_root/.tmp_task10_probe_xxx). Since it's inside the module, it can import internal/builder. Good.\n\nBut wait — is `.tmp_task10_probe_xxx` (starting with `.`) ignored by `go build ./...`? Yes, dot-prefixed dirs are ignored. But `go build -o probe_bin .` from INSIDE that dir explicitly builds it. Good.\n\nNow the sequence for exception:\n```python\ngrilling_path = os.path.join(repo_root, 'internal', 'prompt', 'templates', 'skills', 'grilling.md')\nbackup = read_bytes(grilling_path)  # need binary-safe\ntry:\n    os.remove(grilling_path)\n    # rebuild probe (grilling absent)\n    rc_build, out_build, err_build = run([go, 'build', '-o', probe_bin, '.'], cwd=probe_dir)\n    if rc_build != 0:\n        errors.append('... build failed after deleting grilling.md ...')\n    else:\n        rc, out, err = run([probe_bin], cwd=repo_root, env=probe_env)\n        # check\n        if rc != 0:\n            errors.append('BuildPlan panicked/crashed (exit=%d) instead of returning error')\n        elif 'grilling' not in out.lower():\n            errors.append('BuildPlan did not return grilling error; got: ...')\n        elif 'ERR:' not in out:\n            errors.append('... returned something but no error marker ...')\n        # else pass\nfinally:\n    # restore grilling.md\n    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(grilling_path), exist_ok=True)\n    write_bytes(grilling_path, backup)\n```\n\nWait, I need to think about the \"ERR:\" marker and \"NO_ERROR\". Currently (task10 not impl), BuildPl